control &= ~CONTROL_CLKIDLE;
```
```
ctlr->num_chipselect = 2;
```
Reason:
The hardware automatically asserts the built-in chip select when data is written to the transmit buffer. The driver did not disable this automatic behavior (by not setting the software‑control bit in the DIRECT_ACCESS register) and did not manually control the CS. As a result, the built‑in CS was asserted during any transmit, interfering with transfers intended for GPIO‑controlled devices. Additionally, the old setup callback only set basic control flags and did not configure the CS, and the hard‑coded `num_chipselect` assumed multiple CS without the necessary software management.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
val = readl_relaxed(qspi->regs + REG_DIRECT_ACCESS);
val |= DIRECT_ACCESS_EN_SSEL;
writel(val, qspi->regs + REG_DIRECT_ACCESS);
```
```
val &= ~DIRECT_ACCESS_OP_SSEL;
val |= !enable << DIRECT_ACCESS_OP_SSEL_SHIFT;
writel(val, qspi->regs + REG_DIRECT_ACCESS);
```
```
mchp_coreqspi_set_cs(mem->spi, true);
// ... existing transfer code ...
mchp_coreqspi_set_cs(mem->spi, false);
```
Reason:
Setting `DIRECT_ACCESS_EN_SSEL` switches the built‑in chip select to software‑controlled mode, preventing it from being automatically asserted on every transmit. The `mchp_coreqspi_set_cs` function then allows explicit control of the CS line (active low/high). In `mchp_coreqspi_exec_op`, the CS is asserted before the operation and deasserted afterwards, ensuring the built‑in CS is only active when the correct device is being accessed. This avoids interference with other devices that use separate GPIO chip selects.

